Nuvoton Buildroot (buildroot-nvt)
================================

Overview
--------

buildroot-nvt is Nuvoton's Buildroot-based BSP for Nuvoton processors.
It carries Nuvoton platform support and integration patches on top of the
official Buildroot project. Although the initial enablement is focused on
the MA35 series, this repository is not limited to a single Nuvoton SoC.

Maintenance policy
------------------

Nuvoton intends to maintain this repository as a single, periodically
updated downstream tree based on meaningful official Buildroot stable
releases. It will not track every change made to the upstream master
branch. Depending on available resources, product requirements, and the
importance of an upstream release, synchronization is expected roughly
every one to three months.

During an update, Nuvoton-specific changes will be forward-ported to the
selected upstream stable release and validated before a Nuvoton release is
tagged. Release tags will identify the corresponding upstream Buildroot
baseline.

Current status
--------------

This repository is under development and has not yet been formally
released. At present, only a subset of the MA35D1 defconfig configurations
has completed build testing. Other defconfigs and Nuvoton platforms in
this tree must be considered unverified until their validation is explicitly
documented in a release.

The historical board lists and usage notes later in this file are retained
as BSP reference material. They do not represent the validation status of
the current buildroot-nvt development tree.

Upstream Buildroot README
=========================

Buildroot is a simple, efficient and easy-to-use tool to generate embedded
Linux systems through cross-compilation.

The documentation can be found in docs/manual. You can generate a text
document with 'make manual-text' and read output/docs/manual/manual.text.
Online documentation can be found at https://buildroot.org/docs.html

To build and use the buildroot stuff, do the following:

1) run 'make menuconfig'
2) select the target architecture and the packages you wish to compile
3) run 'make'
4) wait while it compiles
5) find the kernel, bootloader, root filesystem, etc. in output/images

You do not need to be root to build or run buildroot.  Have fun!

Buildroot comes with a basic configuration for a number of boards. Run
'make list-defconfigs' to view the list of provided configurations.

